{
 "esm": "1.1.0",
 "metadata": {
  "name": "NoahFrozenSoilColumnDew",
  "description": "The frozen soil column of WRF's unified Noah land-surface model on a dewing column -- vertical water transport and the freeze/thaw phase change summed against WRF's own step. WHY A COUPLING DOCUMENT AND NOT A COMPONENT TEST. Each half is already pinned on its own: `soil_moisture.esm` reproduces SMFLX/SRT as Richards' equation, and `frozen_soil.esm` reproduces TBND/TMPAVG/FRH2O/SNKSRC with every assertion exact at zero tolerance since the N97 hook. Neither, alone, says anything about the quantity a consumer actually integrates, which is their SUM -- and WRF never forms that sum. SFLX calls SMFLX, which advances the liquid water over the step, and only then SHFLX -> HRT, whose phase-change sink acts on the water SMFLX has ALREADY MOVED. WRF's answer is a sequential composition of two operators; a continuous formulation is their sum evaluated at ONE instantaneous state. That the two agree is a claim about the pair, it is O(dt), and it has to be measured. This document builds one column state, hands it to BOTH mounts, and asserts the sum against WRF's own (sh2o_out - sh2o_in)/dt. THREE CLAIMS, NOT ONE. (1) The liquid budget: transport + phase = WRF's total step. (2) The total-water budget: transport ALONE reproduces WRF's smc step, because the phase change moves water between liquid and ice within a layer and does not change the total -- an independent check that the split was made in the right place, since an error leaking transport into the phase term would pass (1) and fail (2). (3) The clamp decision: `phase_active`, decided at the shared state, matches at ZERO tolerance the decision WRF made at its post-SMFLX state. (3) is what licenses (1): the phase rate is qtot/(rho_w hlice dz) where the sink runs and zero where the interval clamp holds the layer, so it depends on the liquid water ONLY through that discrete switch, and if the switch does not flip between the two states the splitting error cannot enter through it at all. THE REFERENCE STEP IS A FLOOR CASE (FORTRAN_BUGS N99, PLAN section 6) and its V was measured over all seventeen cold bare-ground columns rather than guessed. Below: the reference (sh2o_out - sh2o_in)/dt is a difference of two nearly equal stored states, and the increment is only 3e-13 to 2e-11 of sh2o at a 1e-6 s step -- four digits left in binary64. Above: at WRF's own 60 s step the interval clamp decision FLIPS at one layer of six of the seventeen columns (0 -> 2, stopped at `free`), which turns the phase term into a finite-step artefact rather than a rate, exactly the bind FORTRAN_BUGS N97 describes. The minimum is ESM_DT = 0.01 s, where the increment is 3e-9 to 2e-7 of sh2o -- about eight digits -- and every clamp decision still agrees with the one at 1e-6 s. The tolerances are therefore the REFERENCE's cancellation floor, not the assembly's accuracy. SCOPE. `qtot` is consumer-supplied, exactly as in `frozen_soil.esm`'s own tests, so this document isolates the WATER budget; the heat budget that produces qtot belongs to the soil-heat component and joins in a larger assembly. The snow-covered SNOPAC columns are out of scope: there the water supply includes snowmelt, which brings the snow-energy component in as a third mount. THIS COLUMN: case A step 1, the only step in the whole cold reference set that takes the ACTIVE branch of the frozen-ground infiltration reduction (column ice 0.19 m against the 1e-2 m threshold), and one whose water supply is DEW rather than precipitation -- NOPAC adds the dew to the precipitation before the canopy split, so the column has a positive water input with no rain at all. A different transport regime from the case-D columns, on a different case, which is why it is a separate document rather than another assertion.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "tags": [
   "wrf",
   "land-surface",
   "noah",
   "coupling",
   "soil-moisture",
   "frozen-soil",
   "operator-splitting"
  ],
  "references": [
   {
    "citation": "Koren, V., Schaake, J., Mitchell, K., Duan, Q.-Y., Chen, F., & Baker, J. M. (1999). A parameterization of snowpack and frozen ground intended for NCEP weather and climate models. J. Geophys. Res., 104(D16), 19569-19585."
   },
   {
    "citation": "Ek, M. B., et al. (2003). Implementation of Noah land surface model advances in the NCEP operational mesoscale Eta model. J. Geophys. Res., 108(D22), 8851."
   },
   {
    "citation": "wrf-model/WRF v4.8.0 phys/module_sf_noahlsm.F: SFLX's SMFLX call followed by SHFLX -> HRT -> SNKSRC, the sequential composition this document sums instead."
   }
  ]
 },
 "metaparameters": {
  "NLEV": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 4,
   "description": "Number of soil layers, WRF num_soil_layers. Declared at document level and bound into BOTH mount edges, so the two components share one vertical grid."
  }
 },
